(* ============================================================================
   PART 4: ATTRIBUTES
   ============================================================================ *)

attributes = '{', attr_pad, attribute_list, attr_pad, '}' ;
attribute_list = attribute, {space+, attribute} ;

(* THE INLINE SLOT IS A RUN of glued blocks -- see AUTHORED ATTRIBUTES RENDER
   IN SOURCE ORDER below for how the blocks merge. Every inline production
   in this grammar that has an attribute slot spells it `[attribute_run]`.
   The list-marker slot, a table row or cell, and a citation definition line
   keep a single `attributes`. Editorial substitution, editorial comment and
   braced comment take no slot at all (CARVE-P3-017). *)
attribute_run = attributes, {attributes} ;
(* interior padding is accepted: `{ .a }` and `{.a  .b}` are valid inline
   attribute blocks. The terminal is `space`, NOT `whitespace`: see
   ATTRIBUTE PADDING IS SPACE-ONLY below. block_attributes takes the same
   terminal and also admits a line break. *)
(* ATTRIBUTE PADDING IS SPACE-ONLY -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P4-001]. Every slot
   inside an attribute block that sits within a line takes `space`: the run
   after `{`, the run between two attributes, the run before `}`, the boundary
   after an unquoted value, and the blessed empty block `{ }`. That holds for
   the inline block and for `block_attributes` alike, because A TAB IS SYNTAX
   ONLY IN THE LEADING RUN (PART 7). `{.a<TAB>.b}` on a line of its own is
   paragraph text.

   `block_attributes` adds one position: after a `continuation`, the next
   line's leading run IS indentation, measured in columns, and takes
   `whitespace`.

   A newline inside a quoted value is governed by A QUOTED VALUE STOPS AT THE
   NEWLINE below. *)
(* AUTHORED ATTRIBUTES RENDER IN SOURCE ORDER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P4-002]: attributes are emitted in the order they
   appear in the source, with all classes merged into a single `class`
   attribute placed at the position of the FIRST class. So `{.a #b k=c}`
   -> `class="a" id="b" k="c"` and `{k=c .a #b}` -> `k="c" class="a"
   id="b"`. (Block-attribute merge keeps each slot at its first-appearance
   position; values are last-wins, PART 9 §15.) Repeated class VALUES are
   DEDUPLICATED, keeping first-occurrence order: `{.a .a .b}` -> `class="a
   b"`, and classes accumulated across adjacent/continued attribute blocks
   dedup the same way. A class an element adds itself (a typed div's kind
   word, an admonition's `admonition` and type word) is in the same list, so
   `{.note}` over `::: note` renders `class="admonition note"` (PART 9 §12).

   THAT INCLUDES A GLUED RUN ON ONE INLINE ELEMENT. `attribute_run` is one or
   more `attributes` with nothing between them, and the run is ONE list for
   this rule: the blocks merge exactly as PART 9 §15 A3 merges stacked block
   lists, the same merge CARVE-P3-007 applies to a definition's list and the
   reference site's. So `*x*{.k}{.j}` -> `<strong class="k j">`,
   `*x*{#i}{#j}` -> `id="j"`, `*x*{k=1}{k=2}` -> `k="2"`, and `*x*{.k}{.k}`
   -> `class="k"`. The run ENDS at the first thing that is not an attribute
   block: a space (`*x*{.k} {.j}` attaches only `.k`), a brace group that is
   not a valid block (`{???}`, `{ }`), or a glued construct such as the
   forced span in `*x*{.k}{*y*}`, which stays content. A bracketed span is
   the one carrier whose run also takes the empty block `{}` / `{ }` as a
   member (PART 3 `span_attribute_run`). A run that attaches to
   nothing is text, block by block (PART 9 §15 A7). *)

attribute = language_attribute | id_attribute | class_attribute
          | key_value_attribute | boolean_attribute ;

(* `{:TAG}` is EXACT SUGAR for `key_value_attribute` `lang=TAG`, and the empty
   `{:}` for `lang=""`. It desugars during attribute parsing, so there is no
   new AST node, no new AST field, and a consumer that has never heard of the
   shorthand sees an ordinary `lang` key/value.

   `{:}` is not the same as omitting the attribute. Omitting it leaves the
   content inheriting a surrounding language; `{:}` declares the language
   explicitly unknown and stops that inheritance. It is also distinct from
   `{:und}` (BCP 47 `und`, undetermined) and `{:zxx}` (no linguistic content).

   PARSING CHECKS THE STRUCTURAL ENVELOPE ONLY, never a registry: conformance
   must not depend on the IANA Language Subtag Registry, which changes. So
   subtags are ASCII alphanumerics, one to eight characters, hyphen-separated,
   with no empty subtag and no leading or trailing hyphen. Tags are stored
   EXACTLY as authored -- BCP 47 comparison is case-insensitive but parsing and
   formatting do not change case. A candidate that fails this envelope is not
   a partially consumed attribute: the whole block stays literal (PART 9 §14),
   so `{:en_US}`, `{:en--GB}`, `{:-en}` and `{:français}` are literal source.

   LANGUAGE NEVER IMPLIES WRITING DIRECTION. No tag produces `dir` in either
   direction. Authors use HTML's independent
   mechanism explicitly where needed: `[…]{:ar dir=rtl}` or `dir=auto`.

   The deferred braced-symbol spelling `{:name:}` stays literal under this
   production, because a trailing `:` is not a subtag character -- see
   `docs/dismissed-syntax.md`, which reserves `{:` for that form. The two are
   distinguishable (a symbol ends `:}` and a language tag cannot).

   THE SIGIL TAKES NO PADDING -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P4-003]: the tag is optional, not the
   separator, so `{: fr}` is the empty language attribute followed by a
   SEPARATE boolean `fr`, giving `lang="" fr=""`. It is not a language
   attribute reading `fr`, and it does not fail the block. This follows from
   `':', [ language_tag ]` with no special case.

   LANGUAGE SHORTHAND USES THE `lang` KEY -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P4-004]: `:TAG` and `lang=TAG`
   ARE THE SAME KEY, so writing
   both is a repeated key and nothing more. RENDER ORDER above already decides
   it: the LAST value wins and the slot stays at the FIRST appearance. That
   holds inside one block and across merged block-attribute lines, because the
   shorthand desugars before either merge sees it. So `{:fr lang=de}` and
   `{lang=de :fr}` are `lang="de"` and `lang="fr"`; `{k=1 :fr lang=de}` is
   `k="1" lang="de"`, with `lang` in the slot `:fr` opened. This clause adds no
   additional merge rule; it makes the shorthand's expansion explicit.

   CANONICAL FORM: the writer COLLAPSES a repeated `lang` to the one attribute
   that survives the merge, and spells it `:TAG` -- `{:fr lang=de}` formats to
   `{:de}`. An empty value is `{:}`, which is therefore the canonical spelling
   of `{lang=""}` too. Both follow from PART 11's rule that the writer emits
   the canonical spelling of what the AST holds, and the AST holds one `lang`.

   A BOOLEAN `lang` IS THE SAME KEY AS WELL: `{lang}` means `lang=""` under
   PART 4's boolean rule, so `{:fr lang}` ends at `lang=""` and `{lang :fr}` at
   `lang="fr"`. The three spellings of the key meet in one slot, and the corpus
   pins the boolean form beside the other two. *)
language_attribute = ':', [ language_tag ] ;
language_tag       = language_subtag, { '-', language_subtag } ;
language_subtag    = ( letter | digit ), { letter | digit } ;
(* One to eight characters per subtag. *)

id_attribute = '#', explicit_identifier ;
class_attribute = '.', explicit_identifier ;
(* A `class` KEY-VALUE IS A SPELLING OF THE CLASS SLOT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P4-007].
   `class=VALUE` and `.VALUE` are the same attribute, so a parser APPENDS the
   value to the AST's `attrs.classes` in source order and writes nothing to
   `attrs.keyValues`: `{class=foo}` and `{.foo}` parse to one tree. The two
   spellings are not interchangeable in source, because `class_attribute` reads
   the `explicit_identifier` the fence word does while `attribute_value` reaches
   past it: `-col` and `w-1/2` are classes only the key-value form can spell.
   PART 4's three spellings of `lang` likewise share one slot.

   THE VALUE IS A LIST OF CLASS TOKENS. A parser splits the value on ASCII
   whitespace (space, tab, LF, FF, CR) and appends each token as its own
   class, the way HTML reads a `class` attribute, so the CARVE-P4-002 dedup
   compares tokens: `{class="a b" .a}` parses to the classes `a`, `b` and
   renders `class="a b"`. A value that holds no token
   (`class=""`) stays one empty entry. Each token is an authored value of its
   own for CARVE-P9-054. *)
key_value_attribute = identifier, '=', attribute_value ;
(* A bare identifier is a VALUE-LESS BOOLEAN attribute in source. It has the same
   empty-string AST value as `name=""` and renders `name=""` (a carve
   extension beyond canonical djot, matching
   djot-php; matched after key_value_attribute so `k=v` is not read as a bare
   `k`). It mixes freely with the other forms. See PART 9 §14. *)
boolean_attribute = identifier - ('_', {character}) ;
(* A FEW BOOLEAN NAMES ARE CONSUMED RATHER THAN EMITTED -- the attribute
   vocabulary, listed once so a reader looking for "which names mean something"
   finds them from the production rather than from three separate sections:

     loose         on a LIST or a DEFINITION LIST: the container's children
                   render as blocks rather than inline runs (PART 9 §17 L7)
     header-rows   on a PIPE TABLE, with `footer-rows`: the leading and
     footer-rows   trailing row ranges become `thead` / `tfoot`, boolean
                   meaning one (PART 12 §15)

   `body-rows`, `body-header-rows` and `body-header-cols` (PART 12 §15),
   and `aligns`, `valigns` and `widths` (PART 12 §19) are the same class in
   key/value form. Every other name reaches the output as the attribute it is,
   and each of these does too on a block that has nothing to consume it: the
   name is not reserved, it is read by the block below it. *)
(* A BOOLEAN ATTRIBUTE DOES NOT START WITH AN UNDERSCORE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P4-005].
   `identifier` admits a leading `_`, so without this rule `{_x_}` reads both
   as the boolean attribute `_x_` and as a forced underline. The underline is
   what an author writing `{_x_}` means, so the BARE form gives the collision
   up. There is no boolean attribute to lose: HTML's set
   is `disabled`, `checked`, `readonly`, `required`, `hidden` and their kind.

   ONLY THE BARE FORM IS NARROWED. `{#_id}`, `{._class}` and `{_k=1}` all keep
   their leading underscore, and none of them can be read as an underline
   because none of them ends `_}`. The one underscore attribute in the wild,
   hyperscript's `_="on click …"`, is a key/value and is unaffected.

   `{_foo}` is therefore text: it has no underline reading either, so it
   renders literally. *)

attribute_value = unquoted_value | quoted_value ;
unquoted_value = ( character - '}' - '|' - '"' - "'" - '\' - ' ' - '\t' - newline )+ ;
(* THE EXCLUSIONS ARE WHAT WOULD END OR RESTRUCTURE THE BLOCK, and quoting is
   required for exactly those: `}` closes the block, a space is the separator
   before the next attribute and a tab is no part of a block, a quote opens a
   quoted value, a bare `|`
   would end a table cell, and a `\` needs the quoted form, the only one that
   resolves escapes. Everything else needs
   no quoting, so a version string (`v1.2`), a namespaced token (`xml:lang`) and
   an imported utility class (`w-1/2`) are all bare values. *)
(* Escapes in a quoted value: CARVE-P7-004. `{k="a\}b"}` carries `a}b` and
   `{k="a\zb"}` carries `a\zb`. The lookahead makes a backslash before
   punctuation an escape only; it cannot also be ordinary content before a
   closing quote. *)
literal_backslash = '\', !(ascii_punctuation) ;
quoted_value = '"', { escaped_char | literal_backslash | (character - '"' - '\' - newline) }, '"'
             | "'", { escaped_char | literal_backslash | (character - "'" - '\' - newline) }, "'" ;
(* A QUOTED VALUE STOPS AT THE NEWLINE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P4-006].
   `block_attributes` reads the same `quoted_value`, so a line break inside
   a quoted value ends that block too. A block attribute may still span
   lines: `continuation` is where a newline is admitted, and it sits
   BETWEEN tokens, never inside one. *)

(* Block-level attributes appear on their own line(s) preceding a block.
   A single attribute block may span multiple physical lines (the `}`
   need not be on the opening line): a continuation is a single line
   break optionally followed by indentation. A BLANK line (two line
   breaks) ends the block -- it is not interior padding; an attribute
   block interrupted by a blank line is not a `block_attributes`; its lines
   are literal paragraph text. Merge + reach semantics
   (including float-across-blank-lines BETWEEN separate blocks): PART 9 §15. *)
(* Continuation lines may sit at any column but must carry the quote prefix
   of the container that opened the block. A line without that prefix does
   not continue the attribute: its first line is paragraph text, and the
   prefix-less line may lazily continue that paragraph. *)
block_attributes = '{', opt_pad, attribute, {attr_separator, attribute}, opt_pad, '}', newline
                 | attributes, attributes, {attributes}, newline ;
(* A GLUED RUN IS ONE PHYSICAL LINE. `{.a}{.b}` on a line of its own is one
   attribute line whose blocks merge as PART 9 §15 A3 merges stacked lists.
   Only a lone block may wrap: the second alternative is built from
   `attributes`, whose padding admits no line break, so `{.a}{.b` + newline +
   `}` and `{.a` + newline + `}{.b}` are paragraph text. *)
(* THE PADDING ADMITS A LINE BREAK TOO, not only the separator: `{` + newline
   + `.a}`, `{.a` + newline + `}` and `{.a` + newline + `.b}` are all block
   attributes, and the paragraph below takes `class="a"` or `class="a b"`
   accordingly. The INLINE `attributes` production pads with
   `attr_pad = {space}` alone, which admits no line break. *)
(* block_attributes requires AT LEAST ONE attribute -- there is NO block-level
   blessed-empty form (unlike the inline `[text]{}` span, which is blessed).
   A bare `{}` line is therefore NOT a block-attribute block; it stays a
   literal paragraph (`<p>{}</p>`). *)
attr_pad = {space} ;                     (* attribute-block padding within a
                                            line: spaces only, no tab and no
                                            line break *)
opt_ws = {whitespace} ;                  (* a LEADING indentation run, spaces
                                            and tabs. Read by continuation
                                            only *)
opt_pad = attr_pad, [continuation] ;     (* block-attribute padding: spaces,
                                            then AT MOST ONE line break plus its
                                            indentation -- a blank line ends the
                                            block and is never interior padding *)
attr_separator = (space | continuation), attr_pad ;  (* one space OR one line break *)
continuation = newline, opt_ws ;         (* a single line break + indent; NOT a blank line *)
(* AN IMPORTER READS A BRACE RUN'S CONTINUATION ONLY WHERE IT IS INDENTED --
   NORMATIVE [CARVE-P4-008]. The `continuation` above is Carve's own, and it
   admits an unindented second line; a source language need not. Djot requires
   a block attribute's later lines to be indented past the run's opening
   column, and that requirement covers every block-position brace run, the
   comment-only form `{% ... %}` included -- one construct, so one rule, not a
   separate reading for comments. Inline attribute and comment runs may span
   unindented lines; this block-position requirement does not apply to them. An importer that meets a block-position brace run whose
   continuation sits at or below the opener's own column takes the whole run as
   ordinary text: `{% x` / `y %}` at column 0 imports as a paragraph carrying
   both lines, and a following `# b` is paragraph text in that same paragraph
   rather than a heading, so the ownership of the block after the run follows
   from this rule and needs none of its own. Indented, `{% x` / `  y %}` is a
   comment whose text is dropped, exactly as `{#id` / `  key=1}` applies
   attributes where `{#id` / `key=1}` does not. Inside a container the column
   compared is the opener's, so a list item's content column governs there.
 *)
